# Domain Actions

An Action is a single business use case. One class, one `ExecuteAsync`.

## Shape

- Name: `CreateXAction`, `GetXAction`, `ListXAction`, `UpdateXAction`, `DeleteXAction`, `BulkUpdateXStatusAction`.
- Return value: `Task ExecuteAsync(CreateXDto dto)` for commands that only need a success message; `Task<TResponse> ExecuteAsync(TRequest req)` when data is returned.
- Inputs/outputs: **only** types from `LoadlyAggregator.Domain` and `LoadlyAggregator.Shared`. No `HttpContext`, no `IActionResult`, no API Request/Resource types.
- Dependencies: inject Domain interfaces (repositories, services, filter utilities) via primary constructor; use `IUnitOfWork.SaveChangesAsync()` to persist.

## Guard & throw

Fail fast with Shared exceptions carrying i18n keys:

```csharp
var admin = await _adminRepository.GetByIdAsync(dto.Id)
    ?? throw new NotFoundException("admins.not_found_with_id", dto.Id);

if (contract.Status != ContractStatus.APPROVED)
    throw new ValidationException("contracts.must_be_approved_before_invoicing");
```

Use `NotFoundException`, `ValidationException`, `UnauthorizedException`, `ForbiddenException`, `LogicalException`, `TooManyRequestsException` from `LoadlyAggregator.Shared.Exceptions`.

## Keep it flat

- No long `if/else` chains — extract Strategy/Builder/Resolver or private methods (`ValidateX`, `LoadX`, `BuildResponse`).
- Push query filtering into an `XFilterUtility` for List actions.
- Group multi-step work into private methods; `ExecuteAsync` should read like a short recipe.

## Anti-patterns

- ❌ Taking `CreateAdminRequest` directly from the API layer — always consume `CreateAdminDto`.
- ❌ Catching exceptions to translate them — let them bubble to the middleware.
- ❌ Returning entities (`Admin`, `Contract`) — return a DTO/Response.
- ❌ Calling `SaveChangesAsync()` multiple times per action without a transaction when work must be atomic.
